## [ISSUE] Recurring cancellation loops per reference but ignores the reference

**Severity:** MEDIUM — The recurring-cancel block now runs once per calendar reference but always deletes only the organizer's first reference of each booking, so it deletes the same events N times and never deletes the other hosts' recurring events.

### The Problem
The new `for (const reference of bookingCalendarReference)` wraps the existing recurring branch. That branch does not use `reference`:
```ts
const promises = bookingToDelete.user.credentials
  .filter((credential) => credential.type.endsWith("_calendar"))
  .map(async (credential) => {
    const calendar = await getCalendar(credential);
    for (const updBooking of updatedBookings) {
      const bookingRef = updBooking.references.find((ref) => ref.type.includes("_calendar"));
```

### Theory / Proof
With a collective booking that has K calendar references (one per host), the loop body runs K times. Each time it iterates the organizer's credentials and calls `deleteEvent` for the first `_calendar` reference of every occurrence. That is K × credentials × occurrences delete calls on the same event ids. The other hosts' references (`references[1..]`) are never deleted, and their credentials (fetched from DB just above) are only used to build an unused `calendar`.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Today: non-recurring cancellation handles every reference correctly. Breaks: cancelling all remaining occurrences of a recurring collective event. The organizer's events are deleted (with redundant 404/410 calls), but every other host keeps all the occurrences in their calendar. That is the same kind of bug the PR fixes for single events.

### Research
The non-recurring branch (`apiDeletes.push(calendar?.deleteEvent(uid, evt, externalCalendarId))`) correctly uses the reference's own credential/uid. The recurring branch should do the same per occurrence.

### Recommendation
Move the recurring handling out of the per-reference loop and delete every calendar reference of every occurrence with its own credential:
```ts
if (bookingToDelete.eventType?.recurringEvent && bookingToDelete.recurringEventId && allRemainingBookings) {
  for (const updBooking of updatedBookings) {
    for (const ref of updBooking.references.filter((r) => r.type.includes("_calendar"))) {
      const cred = await findCredential(ref.credentialId); // user.credentials, then prisma
      const calendar = cred ? await getCalendar(cred) : null;
      apiDeletes.push(calendar?.deleteEvent(ref.uid, evt, ref.externalCalendarId));
    }
  }
} else {
  for (const reference of bookingCalendarReference) { /* existing single-booking path */ }
}
```
(`updatedBookings` must select `credentialId` on references.)

## [OPTIMIZATION] Duplicated credential lookup; collective event creation is fully sequential per host

**Severity:** LOW — collective booking creation latency grows linearly with the number of hosts, and the fallback logic is copy-pasted.

### The Problem
Two nearly identical 25-line blocks (`createAllCalendarEvents` around lines 343-366 and `updateAllCalendarEvents` around lines 516-539) run `prisma.credential.findUnique` and build a `CredentialWithAppName`. In `createAllCalendarEvents` the loop is:
```ts
for (const destination of event.destinationCalendar) {
  ... await prisma.credential.findUnique(...)
  const createdEvent = await createEvent(credential, event, destination.externalId);
```
So each host's DB lookup and external calendar API call run in series. (In `updateAllCalendarEvents` only the DB lookup is sequential; the `updateEvent` promises are pushed un-awaited and already run concurrently.)

### Theory / Proof
For N hosts, creation costs N DB round-trips plus N provider calls back to back. Calendar API calls usually take hundreds of ms, and the booking request waits for all of them. The duplicated blocks can also drift (e.g. a fix for `invalid` credentials applied to only one copy).

### What happens today vs. what will break
Fine for 2 hosts. A collective event with many hosts adds noticeable latency to booking. The base code had only one destination calendar per booking, so this cost is new.

### Research
The `else` branch of the same function already uses `Promise.all` over credentials. Prisma can batch the lookups with `findMany({ where: { id: { in: ids } } })`.

### Recommendation
```ts
private async resolveCredentials(ids: number[]): Promise<Map<number, CredentialWithAppName>> {
  const missing = ids.filter((id) => !this.calendarCredentials.some((c) => c.id === id));
  const fromDb = missing.length
    ? await prisma.credential.findMany({ where: { id: { in: missing } }, include: { app: { select: { slug: true } } } })
    : [];
  const map = new Map(this.calendarCredentials.map((c) => [c.id, c]));
  for (const c of fromDb) if (c.app?.slug) map.set(c.id, { ...c, appName: c.app.slug });
  return map;
}
// in createAllCalendarEvents:
const creds = await this.resolveCredentials(destinations.flatMap((d) => (d.credentialId ? [d.credentialId] : [])));
createdEvents.push(
  ...(await Promise.all(
    destinations
      .filter((d) => d.credentialId && creds.has(d.credentialId))
      .map((d) => createEvent(creds.get(d.credentialId!)!, event, d.externalId))
  ))
);
```

