## [ISSUE] `loadUsers` error mapping reports client errors as 500 and DB errors as 400

**Severity:** LOW — The new `loadUsers` try/catch turns all non-Prisma errors, including its own input-validation error, into HTTP 500 "Unable to load users", and turns Prisma DB errors into 400.

### The Problem
```ts
if (!Array.isArray(dynamicUserList) || dynamicUserList.length === 0) {
  throw new Error("dynamicUserList is not properly defined or empty.");
}
...
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof HttpError || error instanceof Prisma.PrismaClientKnownRequestError) {
    throw new HttpError({ statusCode: 400, message: error.message });
  }
  throw new HttpError({ statusCode: 500, message: "Unable to load users" });
}
```

### Theory / Proof
The thrown validation error is a plain `Error`, so it falls to the 500 branch and its message is lost. A `PrismaClientKnownRequestError` is a server/DB failure but is reported as 400 with the raw Prisma message (table/constraint details) sent to the client.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Before: an empty dynamic user list produced an empty `users` array and was rejected later by the existing `users.length` checks with a proper client error. Now a bad dynamic booking URL returns a 500, which pages on-call/alerts as a server fault, and DB outages look like client errors. This refactor is also unrelated to destination calendars.

### Research
HTTP semantics (RFC 9110 section 15.5 / 15.6): 4xx for client-caused errors, 5xx for server failures.

### Recommendation
```ts
if (!Array.isArray(dynamicUserList) || dynamicUserList.length === 0) {
  throw new HttpError({ statusCode: 400, message: "dynamicUserList is not properly defined or empty." });
}
...
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof HttpError) throw error;
  throw new HttpError({ statusCode: 500, message: "Unable to load users" });
}
```
Or revert this block, since it is outside the PR's scope.
